window.__LDNDRC_PAGES = Object.assign(window.__LDNDRC_PAGES || {}, {"what-is-ldndrc":"<p>A robotics workshop needs a room full of gaming laptops and one machine that can show everyone a robot. That normally means a cloud account, a GPU server, and a network engineer. LDNDRC replaces all three with the laptops people already brought.</p>\n<p>The product is a <strong>local-first <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"ros2\" tabindex=\"0\">ROS 2</span> lab</strong>. High-end laptops become compute. Everything else becomes a thin client. The only thing a student installs is nothing — they open a browser.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-clocks\">The three clocks</h2><p>Everything in the system runs on one of three clocks, and keeping them separate is most of the architecture:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clock</th>\n<th>Unit</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Cluster</td>\n<td>minutes</td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">Nodes</span> join, images import, the control panel rolls out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">Session</span></td>\n<td>seconds</td>\n<td>A student asks for a workspace and gets one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Robot</td>\n<td>milliseconds</td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gazebo\" tabindex=\"0\">Gazebo</span> physics and the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"websocket\" tabindex=\"0\">websocket</span> scene stream</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A student never sees the first clock. They see a launch screen, and then they see their robot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-student-actually-gets\">What a student actually gets</h2><p>One login, four hostnames, one private <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>control.ros-platform.local</code> — the panel: login, launch, stop</li>\n<li><code>editor.ros-platform.local</code> — <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"code-server\" tabindex=\"0\">code-server</span>, VS Code with terminals</li>\n<li><code>desktop.ros-platform.local</code> — a full Linux desktop over WebRTC</li>\n<li><code>gazebo.ros-platform.local</code> — the 3D scene, streamed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cluster, the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span>, and the images are invisible. That is the point: the interface is the product.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-is-not\">What it is not</h2><p>It is not a general <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span> tutorial, and it is not a robot simulator. It is the plumbing that turns laptops into a classroom, built so that a single instructor can run it without a cloud bill.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8082/healthz\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># {&quot;status&quot;:&quot;ok&quot;}</span></code></pre></div><p>If that answers, a control panel is alive somewhere and you can start asking it questions. Next: <a href=\"#/roles\" class=\"topic-link\">Hosts, Guests, Operators</a> to see which machine you are standing at.</p>","roles":"<p>There are three roles, and the interesting part is that they are assigned by <strong>hardware</strong>, not by a permission table. A laptop does not choose to be a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"host\" tabindex=\"0\">Host</span>. It either clears the bar or it does not.</p>\n<h2 id=\"host\">Host</h2><p>A gaming or workstation laptop: at least 8 CPU cores, at least 16 GB of RAM, and an NVIDIA GPU. It runs <code>./join-cluster.sh</code>, installs the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"k3s\" tabindex=\"0\">K3s</span> agent, and gets labelled <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/role=host</code>. From then on it is a scheduling target for student <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span>. It should stay plugged in.</p>\n<h2 id=\"guest\">Guest</h2><p>Any laptop with Chrome or Firefox and WebGL. It installs nothing, joins no cluster, and holds no credentials beyond a browser <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span>. It consumes a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> that is scheduled on somebody else&#39;s <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"host\" tabindex=\"0\">Host</span>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"operator\">Operator</h2><p>The master laptop, with a static LAN IP and sleep disabled. It runs the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"k3s\" tabindex=\"0\">K3s</span> server, the control panel, the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"ingress\" tabindex=\"0\">ingress</span>, and the approval console. There is exactly one, and it is the only machine a student never thinks about.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-this-split-works\">Why this split works</h2><p>The three roles map cleanly onto three threat and failure models:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"guest\" tabindex=\"0\">Guest</span></strong> can be compromised without touching anyone else&#39;s data — every <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> is its own <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> with its own home volume.</li>\n<li>A <strong><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"host\" tabindex=\"0\">Host</span></strong> only ever contributes capacity. It runs other people&#39;s <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span> but has no panel credentials.</li>\n<li>The <strong><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">Operator</span></strong> holds the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"jwt\" tabindex=\"0\">JWT</span> secret and the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"k3s\" tabindex=\"0\">K3s</span> token. Losing a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"host\" tabindex=\"0\">Host</span> is an inconvenience; losing the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">Operator</span> is a rebuild.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is also why onboarding is <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">operator</span>-gated: a laptop that clears the hardware bar still cannot become a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"host\" tabindex=\"0\">Host</span> until a human approves it. See <a href=\"#/nodes-api\" class=\"topic-link\">Register, approve, poll</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-the-boundary-actually-sits\">Where the boundary actually sits</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">Guest browser ──cookie──▶ control panel ──JWT──▶ session pod on a Host\n                                │\n                                └─ K3s token ─▶ Host agent (approved once)</code></pre></div><p>A <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"guest\" tabindex=\"0\">Guest</span> never talks to a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"host\" tabindex=\"0\">Host</span> directly. All traffic is HTTP, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"websocket\" tabindex=\"0\">WebSocket</span>, or WebRTC through the panel&#39;s <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span> — which is the next idea, <a href=\"#/dds-isolation\" class=\"topic-link\">Why DDS never leaves the pod</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl get nodes -L node-role.kubernetes.io/role</code></pre></div><p>The <code>ROLE</code> column is the whole role model in one line.</p>","lan-first":"<p>The requirement that shaped everything else: the platform must work with <strong>no cloud, no dedicated server, and no internet dependency</strong> during a workshop.</p>\n<p>That sounds like a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">deployment</span> detail. It is really a design constraint that eliminates whole categories of solution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-rules-out\">What it rules out</h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Managed <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span>.</strong> No EKS, no GKE, no <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"control-plane\" tabindex=\"0\">control plane</span> you do not own.</li>\n<li><strong>External identity.</strong> No OAuth provider. Login has to work when the uplink is down.</li>\n<li><strong>Hosted object storage.</strong> Student home directories live on a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVC</span> in the cluster.</li>\n<li><strong>CDNs.</strong> Fonts, viewer assets, and scripts ship with the image.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-forces\">What it forces</h2><p><strong>A static address.</strong> The master needs a predictable LAN IP, because four hostnames have to resolve to it. There is no DNS server, so <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"guest\" tabindex=\"0\">Guests</span> get <code>/etc/hosts</code> entries or <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"mdns\" tabindex=\"0\">mDNS</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>A hardware tier.</strong> Since compute is donated, the platform must decide which laptops qualify — that is <a href=\"#/hardware-gate\" class=\"topic-link\">The hardware gate</a>, and it exists because of this constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>An enrollment path that works before the network works.</strong> A laptop that has never seen the cluster cannot download an agent from it. The answer is a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"captive-portal\" tabindex=\"0\">captive portal</span> served on a separate onboarding access point: <a href=\"#/captive-portal\" class=\"topic-link\">The captive onboarding portal</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Local images.</strong> A 10 GB <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"workspace-image\" tabindex=\"0\">workspace image</span> cannot be pulled from a registry over a workshop uplink. It is built, tagged immutably, exported as a tarball, and imported <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> by <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-trade-off\">The trade-off</h2><p>You get predictability, zero recurring cost, and data that never leaves the room. You pay in operational sharp edges: IP addresses are manual, image distribution is manual, and the master is a single point of failure with no failover.</p>\n<p>For a classroom that is the right trade. For a product you would sell, it is not — which is why the roadmap keeps <a href=\"#/roadmap\" class=\"topic-link\">What is still left</a> honest about what is still missing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">ip -4 addr show | grep inet</code></pre></div><p>If the master&#39;s address is DHCP-assigned, this constraint is already being violated. Pin it.</p>","dds-isolation":"<p><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"ros2\" tabindex=\"0\">ROS 2</span> uses <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"dds\" tabindex=\"0\">DDS</span> for discovery and message passing. <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"dds\" tabindex=\"0\">DDS</span> is chatty: it multicasts, it discovers peers automatically, and it assumes a flat network. Put twenty student robots on one LAN and every <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> sees every other <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span>.</p>\n<p>The platform&#39;s answer is not a firewall rule. It is a <strong>network <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span></strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-mechanism\">The mechanism</h2><p>A <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span> <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> shares one network <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span> across all its containers. <code>code-server</code>, Selkies, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gazebo\" tabindex=\"0\">Gazebo</span>, and the ROS processes all see the same <code>localhost</code>. <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"dds\" tabindex=\"0\">DDS</span> <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"multicast\" tabindex=\"0\">multicast</span> never crosses the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> boundary because, as far as the kernel is concerned, there is nowhere to cross to.</p>\n<p><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"flannel\" tabindex=\"0\">Flannel</span> — the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cni\" tabindex=\"0\">CNI</span> — carries only HTTP, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"websocket\" tabindex=\"0\">WebSocket</span>, and WebRTC between <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span>. No <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"dds\" tabindex=\"0\">DDS</span> packet ever traverses the overlay.</p>\n<h2 id=\"defense-in-depth\">Defense in depth</h2><p><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">Namespace</span> isolation alone is enough in theory. The platform adds two more layers anyway:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A <strong>unique <code>ROS_DOMAIN_ID</code> per <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span></strong>. Two <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span> that somehow shared a network would still not discover each other&#39;s topics.</li>\n<li><code>ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST</code>, which pins discovery to loopback even inside the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The second layer costs nothing and turns a subtle misconfiguration into a non-event.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-not-just-use-separate-vlans\">Why not just use separate VLANs?</h2><p>VLANs require switch configuration and physical control of the network. The workshop happens on whatever Wi-Fi the venue has. A <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span> is a property of the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">deployment</span>, not of the venue.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-this-buys\">What this buys</h2><ul>\n<li>A student cannot accidentally publish <code>/cmd_vel</code> into another student&#39;s robot.</li>\n<li>Two students can run the same world and the same topic names with no coordination.</li>\n<li>Teardown is <code>DELETE /api/sessions/{id}</code> — the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span> goes with it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cost is that cross-<span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> collaboration is impossible by construction. If two students need to share a topic, they have to share a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span>. That is a deliberate limitation, not a missing feature.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec</span> &lt;session-pod&gt; -- <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">env</span> | grep ROS_DOMAIN_ID\nkubectl -n ldndrc <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec</span> &lt;session-pod&gt; -- <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">env</span> | grep ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE</code></pre></div><p>Compare the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"domain-id\" tabindex=\"0\">domain IDs</span> of two <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span>. They must differ. Then read <a href=\"#/ros-domain-id\" class=\"topic-link\">Unique ROS_DOMAIN_ID per pod</a> for how the value is assigned.</p>"});
